<article class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Reactivity</p>
  <h1>The data seam</h1>
  <p class="lead">deck mounts views but owns no data. Your app exposes named clients through one seam; every view reads them off <code>ctx.clients</code>.</p>

  <h2>The clients seam</h2>
  <p>Declare a clients module in <code>deck.jsonc</code>. deck wires its exports into the router, and every view reads them by name.</p>
  <pre class="code">{configExample}</pre>
  <pre class="code">{usageExample}</pre>

  <h2>Live: durable state</h2>
  <p>This site exposes a <code>store</code> client with a persisted <code>visits</code> counter. It lives <em>above</em> the router, so it survives client-side navigation <strong>and</strong> a full reload — unlike a route signal, which resets when you navigate away.</p>
  <div class="demo">
    <p class="big">{visits()}</p>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" on:click={() => record()}>Record a visit</button>
      <button class="btn ghost" type="button" on:click={() => reset()}>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">Bump it, navigate to another page, come back — the number holds.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Live server data</h2>
  <p>A <code>&lt;data source="…" live&gt;</code> binding subscribes a view to live server data. That transport is <strong>host-wired</strong>: a host (like shipd) mounts it and supplies a resolver. Standalone <code>deck dev</code> serves routing, assets, SSR and HMR but not live server data yet — that's the server-functions work (#32). Use a client store for state until then.</p>

